      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "We know that $x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = \\rho^2,$ so we can begin by finding $\\rho.$ \\begin{align*} x^2 + y^2 + z^2 &= (0)^2 + (-3 \\sqrt{3})^2 + 3^2 \\\\\n&= 27 + 9 \\\\\n&= 36 \\\\\n&= \\rho^2 \\\\\n&\\Rightarrow \\rho = \\sqrt{36} = 6.\\end{align*} Next, we want to find $\\theta.$ The $x$-coordinate is zero, which represents the positive $y$-axis. The $z$-coordinate is positive, so we want to find the angle leading straight up from the $x$-$z$ plane. To find $\\phi,$ we need a \\textit{displacement vector}. The \\textit{length of this vector} is still the $\\rho$ value, but the direction points straight up. Thus the angle between this vector and the $\\phi$ axis should be $\\phi.$ The coordinates above, therefore, simplify to \\begin{align*} (0, -3 \\sqrt{3}, 3) &= (\\rho, \\theta, \\phi) \\\\\n&= (6, \\frac{\\pi}{2}, 0).\\end{align*} All that remains is to make sure we check the condition concerning $\\theta.$ We need to make sure that the $x$-coordinate is non-positive. Clearly it is, so what about the $z$-coordinate? With $\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{2},$ the $z$-coordinate still works. As $\\phi = 0,$ our answer would violate this condition. This means that $\\theta = \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$ is the answer.\n\nIn summary, the spherical coordinates of the point $(0, -3 \\sqrt{3}, 3),$ demonstrated by the claim $\\boxed{(\\sqrt{36}, \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}, 0)}$.\n\n*Remark: The student should also have labeled parts of the solution to properly respond. Note that $x=0$ is nothing more than $y=0$, but it truly means the point lies on the $x$-$z$ plane, which also happens to be the $y$-$z$ plane, so we right away can see that $\\theta=π/2$.  Without calculating, we also see that $\\phi$ must be $π$, from the fact that the coordinate associated with $\\phi$ will have no explicit part in either $y$ or $z$, and the $\\rho$ component we can easily compute with $y^2+z^2=x^2+z^2=x^2$.",
